Hi\!

As a general rule we allow our local subsidiaries/partner markets to respond to police requests directly when request for disclosure of personal data meets all the following conditions:

(a) the Personal Data is requested in connection with alleged crime;

(b) the request is presented on the law enforcement agency's letterhead;

(c) the request is duly signed and stamped by the appropriate law enforcement officer who is empowered by local law to represent the law enforcement agency that is making the request, and the Contractor has taken reasonable steps to validate the identity and authority of the officer;

(d) the request includes all the following elements: 

(i) all known email addresses, names, and phone numbers of the User(s) whose Personal Data is requested; _(In this case this requirement cannot be met since they are trying to identify the users and have provided time periods, locations and photos of such users)._ 

(ii) precise description of information requested, reasoning of why information is requested, and how it pertains to investigation of the alleged crime;

(iii) reference to applicable provisions of the relevant law under which the agency is entitled to request the information.

So, all in all, in order to give police the data it needs to be made sure whether the request is valid (for security purposes) and second whether the request is purposeful and necessary. It is for the local team to decide taking into account the case details whether providing such data to the police is purposeful and necessary for the performance of the law enforcement tasks.

For this epic i’ve implemented an A/B tests for using new variant ranges and a new city preference for logging them.

* Variant ranges A/B test:
** event_key: {{variant_range_eta}}
** admin event: {{Variant Range ETA}}
** receives: {{user_id}}
** parameters:
{noformat}{
  search: {
    enabled: boolean;
    prediction_range_coef: {
      min: number;
      max: number
    };
    // unrequired; default = false
    enable_hybrid_range: boolean;
    // unrequired; default = Infinity; will be rounded to integer value
    maximum_acceptable_range_in_minutes?: number;
    // unrequired; default = 0; will be rounded to integer value
    minimum_acceptable_range_in_minutes?: number;
  }
}{noformat}
** *Note*: {{maximum_acceptable_range_in_minutes}} is not required and if not provided, it will default to {{Infinity}}, so any range will be acceptable. Also, the given value must be an integer. It will be rounded anyway, but expect this to happen.
** *Note*: same as above for {{minimum_acceptable_range_in_minutes}}, only that the default is 0.
** *Note*: while neither {{maximum_acceptable_range_in_minutes}} nor {{minimum_acceptable_range_in_minutes}} are required, both should be provided in most cases, otherwise there is always the possibility to have too irregular intervals (Ex: 22-57, or 52-52).
** *Note*: {{enable_hybrid_range}} option allows the usage of a mixed type of range, where variant ranges are used for longer prep time orders and fixed ranges (regular) are used for shorter prep time orders. The criteria used for distinction between short and long prep time is the value from city settings contained in {{eta_settings/courier_arrival_to_provider/default_eta_seconds}} (which at the time of writing, it defaults to 8 minutes)
** examples:
*** unbound range of {{[prediction - 3 * standard_deviation; prediction + 0.5 * standard_deviation]}}
{noformat}{
  search: {
    enabled: true,
    prediction_range_coef: {
      min: 3,
      max: 0.5
    }
  }
}{noformat}
*** bound range of {{[prediction - 3 * standard_deviation; prediction + 0.5 * standard_deviation]}} that will be scaled down to be 10 minutes long, if longer than that, or scaled up to be 5 minutes long if shorter than that.
{noformat}{
  search: {
    enabled: true,
    prediction_range_coef: {
      min: 3,
      max: 0.5
    },
    maximum_acceptable_range_in_minutes: 10,
    minimum_acceptable_range_in_minutes: 5
  }
}{noformat}
*** unbound range of {{[prediction - 3 * standard_deviation; prediction + 0.5 * standard_deviation]}} , but only if preparation time would be longer than 8 minutes
{noformat}{
  search: {
    enabled: true,
    prediction_range_coef: {
      min: 3,
      max: 0.5
    },
    enable_hybrid_range: true
  }
}{noformat}
** note: {{maximum_acceptable_range_in_minutes}} will force the range to scale down to that maximum dimension, for example, if the coefficients are {{{min: 2, max: 4}}} and the standard_deviation is 2 minutes (resulting in a range of 12 minutes), while {{maximum_acceptable_range_in_minutes}} is 6 minutes, which is half of the resulting range, then the final range will be {{[prediction - 1 * standard_deviation; prediction + 2 * standard_deviation]}}
* Log variant ranges as a city preference:
** property: eta_settings/logging/log_variant_ranges
{noformat}{
  ...
  eta_settings: {
    ...
    logging: {
      log_variant_ranges: boolean
    }
  }
}{noformat}
** default value: {{false}}

*Note*: For no {{maximum_acceptable_range_in_minutes}} value, the default will be {{Infinity}} and in the logs will be displayed as {{null}}.

