Hi [~accountid:602f9aeb871d30006843505c] 

 

The external counsels have answered the questions we asked them about the new Electronic HR Registry in Spain. Please see below a summary of external counsel’s advice and see attached the emails exchanged with them: 

 

As to whether Bolt will be considered as transport provider if Bolt submits the information on behalf of the drivers via one of its legal entities and whether or not provision of this service will have legal or tax implications for us, the lawyers say that: 

# {color:#36b37e}The intermediation in VTC activities, which is Bolt’s business, {color}{color:#36b37e}*is not subject to any regulatory requirement in terms of permits, licenses or authorisations.*{color}{color:#36b37e} {color}
# {color:#36b37e}Currently, every car carrying out VTC services must hold an authorisation, but no other authorisation is required for the intermediation, as this is currently a liberalised activity. {color}
# {color:#36b37e}The fact that Bolt could be considered as a provider of information society services or an intermediary of transport services does not change the fact that {color}{color:#36b37e}*no authorisation*{color}{color:#36b37e} is needed from a transport perspective, as the intermediation in the activity of transport of passengers {color}{color:#36b37e}*is not subject to authorisation.*{color}{color:#36b37e} {color}

 

As to whether Bolt would need a PoA to submit the information on behalf of the drivers/fleet partners, the external counsels say that: 

# {color:#36b37e}There is no information about how this authorisation will be implemented in practice. The general principle applicable to PoAs in favour of representatives to act electronically before Public Administrations is that no formal PoA is required. The PoA to be granted by the drivers/fleet should be just an online process which should not require a notarial deed. {color}

 

As to whether the communication of such data on behalf of the drivers/fleet partners could have any implications from a data privacy perspective, the external lawyers say that: 

# {color:#36b37e}To better answer this question, they would need to carry out a specific review of the agreements executed by Bolt with VTC license holders to see how the data protection regime is settled, but their understanding is that Bolt already has the personal data subject to notification as data controller (car plate / holder, if an individual, of the VTC license / location of the driver if he/she is the one holding the VTC license).  {color}
# {color:#36b37e}They understand that the other data to be notified, on behalf of the VTC license holders would be non-personal data. However, they believe that the execution of a potential data processing agreement with the VTC license holders should be evaluated, as the processing of that personal data would not be for Bolt own purposes and, particularly, to limit potential liabilities.{color}
 

As to whether or not we will be liable for in the data communicated on behalf of the fleet partners/drivers to the Electronic Registry through one of our subsidiaries, the external counsels say: 

# {color:#36b37e}As a general rule, the responsible for the potential breaches of the obligation to submit the relevant data lies with the VTC driver. This is the rule contained in article 28 of Act 40/2015, on the legal regime of the public sector. {color}
# {color:#36b37e}However, we must distingue between the responsible of the obligation in accordance with the law (i.e. the VTC driver) and the responsible of the obligation based on a private agreement (i.e. Bolt) .  {color}
# {color:#36b37e}In this regard, their recommendation is to regulate this “private obligation” of Bolt in the agreements to be subscribed with fleets/drivers to try, to the maximum extent possible, to hold Bolt harmless. Ideally, a systems by means of which VTC license holders validate the information to be submitted before Bolt sends it out to the competent authorities should be settled. {color}



However, we have sent an email with follow up questions to clarify some points that are not entirely clear to us. Please find below the email we have sent to the lawyers:



_Dear Samuel and Jose,_ 

 

_We would like to ask some follow-up questions if I may:_ 

 

* _Regarding your response to our first question, we understand that the intermediation in VTC activities is not subject to any regulatory requirement in terms of licenses or authorisations. However, we would like to confirm that the fact that we submit the information on behalf of the VTC license does not put us at risk of being reclassified as transport providers. Could you please assure us that submitting the information would not imply a reclassification as a transportation provider?_ 
* _Regarding the data privacy aspect, we will provide you with our Data Privacy Policy later for an in-depth discussion, but we suggest to wait until we learn more details about the process._ 
* _Regarding your answer to our last question, I anticipate that your suggestions for a system whereby VTC license holders validate the information to be submitted before Bolt sends it out is difficult to implement as the information must be submitted immediately after the ride. However, we will discuss this with our business team. As for your recommendation of subscribing an agreement with fleets/drivers to try, to  the maximum extent possible, to hold Bolt harmless, we understand that you talk  about an agreement to exclude our liability to VTC license holders. Is this something  that can be added to the Fleet partners agreements and/or T&Cs instead of rather  than entering into a separate agreement for that purpose?_ 

 

_We look forward to hearing from you._ 

 

_Many thanks in advance,_ 



Once they come back to us, we will arrange a 30 minutes call with you to discuss further.

In the meantime, should you have any questions, please do let us know.

Thank you\!
